   "shape_m": "p",
   "html_text": "Interestingly, both the phenomenological and the analytic traditions that reject the perceptual model emphasize the role of action and bodily know-how in their account of bodily awareness. Anscombe describes knowledge without observation as a form of practical knowledge. In a nutshell, knowing that your legs are crossed is not feeling them to be crossed; it is knowing how to uncross them. Along the same lines, Merleau-Ponty claims that the lived body is a space of possibilities for actions. Ther",
   "md_text": "Interestingly, both the phenomenological and the analytic traditions that reject the perceptual model emphasize the role of action and bodily know-how in their account of bodily awareness. Anscombe describes knowledge without observation as a form of practical knowledge. In a nutshell, knowing that your legs are crossed is not feeling them to be crossed; it is knowing how to uncross them. Along the same lines, Merleau-Ponty claims that the lived body is a space of possibilities for actions. Ther"
